<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/inventions?focus=phonograph">Inventions &amp; Discoveries</a> · Communication and computing</p><h1 class="enc-title">Phonograph</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1877">1877</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as gramophone, record player, turntable</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The phonograph is a device for the mechanical and analogue recording and reproduction of sound, in which a stylus traces a groove cut into a rotating cylinder or disc. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thomas-edison">Thomas Edison</a>, working to play back recorded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/telegraph">telegraph</a> messages and to automate speech for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/telephone">telephone</a>, announced it in November 1877 and patented it in 1878; his first machines recorded on tinfoil wrapped round a grooved cylinder. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-graham-bell">Alexander Graham Bell</a>'s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/volta-river">Volta</a> Laboratory improved it in the 1880s with the graphophone and wax-coated cylinders, and in the 1890s Emile Berliner moved from cylinders to flat discs, naming his machine the gramophone. The disc record was the main commercial audio format for most of the 20th century.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/phonograph.jpg" alt="Phonograph" width="330" height="416" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Levin C. Handy (per) · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Edison_and_phonograph_edit1.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Sound recording began before the phonograph. Édouard-Léon Scott de Martinville invented the phonautograph in 1857, a device that traced sound waves in graphic form on paper for visual study. On 30 April 1877 the French poet and inventor Charles Cros deposited a sealed envelope with the French Academy of Sciences describing how a traced line might be used to reproduce sound.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thomas-edison">Thomas Edison</a> conceived the principle of recording and reproducing sound between May and July 1877, as a by-product of efforts to play back recorded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/telegraph">telegraph</a> messages and to automate speech for transmission by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/telephone">telephone</a>. He announced the invention on 21 November 1877, demonstrated it on 29 November, and patented it on 19 February 1878 as US Patent 200,521. His first machines recorded on tinfoil wrapped around a grooved cylinder, with the stylus indenting the foil in hill-and-dale variations. The first demonstrations astonished onlookers, one of them at the office of Scientific American in December 1877.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>A phonograph records sound vibrations as corresponding deviations in a spiral or helical groove on a rotating cylinder or disc. To play it back, the surface is rotated while a stylus traces the groove and is vibrated by it. In early acoustic machines the stylus drove a diaphragm that sent sound through a flaring horn, or through stethoscope-type earphones. Terms differ by region: in American English, phonograph was properly the name for Edison's machines, and Emile Berliner's disc machine was the gramophone.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>Edison's tinfoil machine was too hard to use well; the foil tore easily, reproduction was distorted and good for only a few playbacks, and he did not at once improve it. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-graham-bell">Alexander Graham Bell</a> and two associates began work at the Volta Laboratory in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/washington-dc">Washington, D.C.</a>, in 1879 and were granted basic patents for recording in wax in 1886. The Volta Graphophone Company was created in January 1886, and American Graphophone, formed in March 1887, later evolved into Columbia Records.</p><p>From 1890 makers began to mass-produce cylinders by duplication, and a pantograph process could make 90 to 150 copies at once. In 1892 Berliner began commercial production of disc records and gramophones, five inches across at first. Discs were not inherently better than cylinders; their advantage was that they could be stamped and their matrices shipped to other plants, while cylinders could not be stamped until Edison's gold moulding process of 1901–1902. In the 1930s vinyl came into use for radio transcription discs, and the disc record was the dominant commercial audio format throughout most of the 20th century. Phonographs were the first home audio equipment that people owned in their residences.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Phonograph” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Thomas Edison spoke the nursery rhyme into his tinfoil <b>phonograph</b> and heard his voice played back.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/december-6">6 December in history</a></li><li><blockquote>Edison patented more than 1,000 inventions, including the <b>phonograph</b> and a practical incandescent lamp.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/february-11">11 February in history</a></li><li><blockquote>Edison's tinfoil <b>phonograph</b> was the first machine able to record sound and play it back.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/february-19">19 February in history</a></li><li><blockquote>Thomas Edison announced his invention of the <b>phonograph</b>, a machine that recorded sound on tinfoil and played it back.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/november-21">21 November in history</a></li><li><blockquote>Louis Glass installed a coin-in-the-slot <b>phonograph</b> at the Palais Royale Saloon in San Francisco.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/november-23">23 November in history</a></li><li><blockquote>Thomas Edison filed a patent caveat describing an 'optical <b>phonograph</b>' to record and reproduce moving images.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/october-17">17 October in history</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="inv-entry"><h2 class="enc-section-title">On the inventions tree</h2><dl class="inv-entry-facts"><div><dt>First known</dt><dd>1877</dd></div><div><dt>Credited to</dt><dd>Thomas Edison</dd></div></dl><p class="inv-entry-note"><span class="inv-k">Note</span>The phonautograph, patented in 1857 by Édouard-Léon Scott de Martinville, recorded sound but could not play it back, and Charles Cros described a way of reproducing sound in a sealed letter of April 30, 1877, which he later had read to claim priority of conception.</p><div class="inv-entry-rel"><span class="inv-k">Built on</span><a class="inv-chip" href="/encyclopedia/telegraph">Electrical telegraph<span>1816</span></a><a class="inv-chip" href="/encyclopedia/telephone">Telephone<span>1861</span></a></div><div class="inv-entry-rel"><span class="inv-k">Led to</span><a class="inv-chip" href="/encyclopedia/magnetic-tape">Magnetic tape<span>1928</span></a><a class="inv-chip" href="/encyclopedia/compact-disc">Compact disc<span>1982</span></a></div><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/inventions?focus=phonograph">See it on the inventions tree &rarr;</a></p></section>
